Important Note Regarding Code Examples
Notice to Readers
All code presented in this documentation is for illustrative purposes only and its sole purpose is to facilitate the conceptual understanding of the mechanisms described.

Original Code
The actual, functional, and actively developing source code is available at:

🔗 https://github.com/1Abc342PedroGina/biblekernel

Differences between the Documentation and the Actual Code
1. Typing Style
Documentation: Uses types like uint16_t, uint32_t (Unix/POSIX style) for reader familiarity

Original: Implements its own type system, without Unix dependencies

2. Data Structures
Documentation: Shows structs with named fields for pedagogical clarity

Original: May use different approaches, optimized for the specific kernel design

3. Naming Conventions
Documentation: Uses intuitive names like task_id_t, process_t

Original: Follows its own conventions defined in the repository

4. APIs and Functions
Documentation: Presents conceptual functions like task_create(), kspc_send()

Original: Implements the actual interfaces, which may have distinct parameters and behaviors.

Purpose of the Documentation
This documentation serves as a conceptual guide to understanding:

The architecture of PROCESSES, TASKS, threads, and activities

The KSPC and KHPC communication mechanisms

The gate + joint model

The hierarchy and relationships between components

The examples are didactic simplifications to convey these ideas as clearly as possible.

For Developers and Contributors
If you are interested in the actual code:

Access the official repository: https://github.com/1Abc342PedroGina/biblekernel

Explore the actual implementation of the concepts discussed

Consult the actual documentation in the repository

Participate in development through issues and pull requests

